        <div class='w-full my-0 mx-auto flex flex-col s:mx-5 s:w-full-minus-25 s:max-w-mod-inline m:max-w-mod-inline l:flex-row l:max-w-mod-large justify-end  s:mb-4'>
                            <div class='caption wysiwyg h5 theme__anchors--solid fill-h  w-auto l:w-140'>
                    <p>With the optical tweezer arrays used in neutral-atom quantum computing, it’s easy to rearrange atoms to bring any pair of qubits into contact. In <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-018-0450-2">a 2018 paper</a>, researchers at Paris-Saclay University showed off this capability by arranging atoms in many striking three-dimensional shapes, including one modeled after the Eiffel Tower.</p>
                </div>
            <div class='attribution theme__anchors--solid wysiwyg pangram h6 mb1 fill-h  text-left w-auto before:block l:before:hidden l:w-half-minus-17 l:text-right l:pl-24'>
    <p>Thierry Lahaye/CNRS</p>
</div>
        </div>
    </section>
